# The warm re-sit of the Qwen3.8 table on the A6000 (lcppwarm), after the Qwen3.8 batched prefill lands.
# Its bench rows were warm (q38.log: timed 0..1) but our round-1 rows read cold PLE rows (timed
# 873..1062 faults, ~10 % slower decode, while the cold bound said 0.2..2.6 %): BLOOMERY_WARM_ROWS=1
# primes each of our arms with a same-id run first. The server twins give the reference side the same
# ids as ours.
#
# Assumed, not measured: our arms run the batched prefill (kind=batch) at 300..800 tok/s for P >= 512,
# where the release sitting's kind=pass ran ~104; a 4096 prompt then takes 5..14 s instead of ~40 s.
# Wall [derived from q38.log]: ours 6 512 1024 3000 4096, each a process of ~8 s load + ~2.5 s steps + its
# prompt (8,638 ids a round, 11..29 s), twice under PRIME: 127..163 s a round; the bench arms lcpp:6
# lcpppp:512 lcpp:1024 lcpp:3000 lcpppp:4096 lcpppp4096:4096 lcppfit:6: ~106 s + the fit's load; the
# server arms lcppsrv:6 :1024 :3000 lcppsrvpp:512 :4096 lcppsrvpp4096:4096 lcppsrvfit:6 lcppsrvppfit:4096:
# ~173 s (a ~7 s load, two requests each); three rounds ~21..23 min;
# five engine blocks under blocks order (ours, lcpp, lcppfit, lcppsrv, lcppsrvfit: depth-qwen3moe.sh
# arm_block), five discards ~4 min (q38.log: ours 59 s at kind=pass, lcpp 50 s; the fit and server ones
# a load and one request or run each): ~25..27 min, under 30 on paper, near it: the lead prices it.
# lcppppfit4096 and its server twin are out: the release sitting's lcppppfit4096 aborted (rc 134, a CUDA
# error in ggml_backend_cuda_graph_compute), which ended the segment.
kind: baseline
question: with our arms primed on their own ids, what is Qwen3.8's decode at depth 1024 on the A6000,
  and do the warm llama.cpp server rows match its warm bench rows?
term: tok/s @ n=96, depth 1024, ours (generate over qwen4exp, the lcg ids, after a same-id PRIME
  process), A6000
unit: tok/s
predict: 39.9..41.9
condition: one lease, three rounds, BLOOMERY_AB_ORDER=blocks, BLOOMERY_WARM_ROWS=1, arms 6 512 1024
  3000 4096 lcpp:6 lcpppp:512 lcpp:1024 lcpp:3000 lcpppp:4096 lcpppp4096:4096 lcppfit:6 lcppsrv:6
  lcppsrv:1024 lcppsrv:3000 lcppsrvpp:512 lcppsrvpp:4096 lcppsrvpp4096:4096 lcppsrvfit:6
  lcppsrvppfit:4096; llama-server built in the mainline tree first (else the server arms are refused
  by name before the lease); each block's DISCARD r0 row present; no counted row [cold] (a COLD row's
  retry clean, else FAIL rc=cold); our rows kind=batch; no [cpu-busy] or [other-busy]; the full output
  to a file first
reason: the band is round 2 and 3's warm d1024 rows, 40.74 and 41.06 (timed 0), +-2 % [derived]; the
  decode step does not move with the batched prefill; why now: round 1's cold PLE rows cost ~10 % and
  the public Qwen3.8 table is re-sat once the batched prefill lands
decide-in: write the Qwen3.8 rows (rig-log first), llama.cpp at the faster of bench and server per
  row, both shown; our pp rows kind=batch
decide-below: the prime did not warm what the row reads: read the PRIME and ROW majflt pair; if the
  row's timed count is still ~900, the faults are not the ids' pages and the PLE read path is the
  next round's question
decide-above: the batched prefill moved a decode term too: diff the step's node count against q38.log
  before the row is published
